Chapter: ArpCacheBound
  cites Net chapter NetworkStack
  cites Net chapter Tcp
  cites Net chapter Ethernet
  cites Foreword chapter Console

 We say:

Section: Fixtures
  srv-mac : List Integer = [82, 84, 0, 18, 52, 86]
  srv-ip : List Integer = [10, 0, 2, 15]
  gw-ip : List Integer = [10, 0, 2, 2]
  gw-mac : List Integer = [82, 85, 10, 0, 2, 2]
  other-ip : List Integer = [10, 0, 2, 99]

  srv0 : NetSession = net-session-new srv-mac srv-ip gw-ip 80 0 [10, 0, 2, 100] 5000

 A distinct sender IP and MAC per frame, which is what an ordinary busy segment
 looks like rather than an attack shape. 300 is past the 0..255 band that used
 to kill the guest and past arp-cache-max.

 THE FLOOD IS REPLIES ADDRESSED TO US, because since 2026-08-20 that is the
 only frame that can enter the cache at all (Damian's ruling, CurrentPlan
 Decisions 2). It used to be requests, which is the shape the narrowing exists
 to refuse, and the capacity question this file is really about would have
 stopped being reachable if the flood had not moved with it. `arp-build-reply`
 puts the sender in the reply-mac and src-ip slots and us in the target slots.

  arp-frame : Integer -> List Integer
  arp-frame (i) =
    let smac = [2, 0, 0, 0, bit-and (bit-shru i 8) 255, bit-and i 255]
    in let sip = [10, 1, bit-and (bit-shru i 8) 255, bit-and i 255]
    in arp-build-reply smac sip srv-mac srv-ip smac

 The old shape, kept as the arm that proves the narrowing rather than deleted:
 a request aimed at a THIRD party, which is a frame we are neither asked to
 answer nor entitled to learn from.

  poison-frame : Integer -> List Integer
  poison-frame (i) =
    let smac = [2, 0, 0, 0, bit-and (bit-shru i 8) 255, bit-and i 255]
    in let sip = [10, 1, bit-and (bit-shru i 8) 255, bit-and i 255]
    in arp-build-request smac sip other-ip

  flood : NetSession, Integer, Integer -> NetSession
  flood (s) (i) (n) =
    if i >= n then s
    else flood ((net-process-frame s (arp-frame i)).session) (i + 1) n

  poison-flood : NetSession, Integer, Integer -> NetSession
  poison-flood (s) (i) (n) =
    if i >= n then s
    else poison-flood ((net-process-frame s (poison-frame i)).session) (i + 1) n

Section: The Arms

 The gateway is learned FIRST and then the flood arrives. It must still be
 there afterwards: that is the whole reason this refuses at capacity instead of
 evicting, and an eviction policy would fail exactly this line.

  gw-learned : NetSession =
    set-arp srv0 (arp-cache-add (srv0.arp) gw-ip gw-mac)

  after-flood : NetSession = flood gw-learned 0 300

 Ten senders sent twice. Before the duplicate check this read 20.

  dup-twice : NetSession = flood (flood srv0 0 10) 0 10

 THE NARROWING, measured rather than asserted. Three hundred requests aimed at
 a third party used to fill this cache to its cap; now they enter nothing and
 queue nothing. The gateway entry learned before the flood is the control: if
 the cache were simply broken rather than narrowed, it would be gone too.

  after-poison : NetSession = poison-flood gw-learned 0 300

 A request aimed at US is answered and STILL teaches us nothing. Both halves
 matter: the reply proves we did not narrow our way out of being a peer, and
 the count proves a forged request cannot put an entry in.

  asked-us : NetResult =
    net-process-frame srv0 (arp-build-request [2, 0, 0, 0, 0, 7] [10, 1, 0, 7] srv-ip)

 arp-cache-search bounds its walk by cache.count and NOT by the length of
 entries, so the two have to move together. A count that runs ahead reads past
 the live entries; one that lags stops short of them and a learned mapping goes
 missing. Neither is visible from a lookup that happens to succeed, so it is
 asserted directly.

Section: Entry
  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
    print-line-uni ("gw-before=" & show (list-length (arp-cache-lookup (gw-learned.arp) gw-ip)))
    print-line-uni ("count-after-300=" & show ((after-flood.arp).count))
    print-line-uni ("capped-at-max=" & show ((after-flood.arp).count == arp-cache-max))
    print-line-uni ("gw-survived-flood=" & show (mac-equal (arp-cache-lookup ((after-flood.arp)) gw-ip) gw-mac))
    print-line-uni ("dup-twice-count=" & show ((dup-twice.arp).count))
    print-line-uni ("dup-updates-not-appends=" & show ((dup-twice.arp).count == 10))
    print-line-uni ("instep-flood=" & show (list-length ((after-flood.arp).entries) == (after-flood.arp).count))
    print-line-uni ("instep-dup=" & show (list-length ((dup-twice.arp).entries) == (dup-twice.arp).count))
    print-line-uni ("poison-count=" & show ((after-poison.arp).count))
    print-line-uni ("poison-taught-nothing=" & show ((after-poison.arp).count == 1))
    print-line-uni ("poison-queued=" & show (list-length (after-poison.outbox)))
    print-line-uni ("poison-gw-intact=" & show (mac-equal (arp-cache-lookup ((after-poison.arp)) gw-ip) gw-mac))
    print-line-uni ("asked-us-output=" & (asked-us.output))
    print-line-uni ("asked-us-queued=" & show (list-length ((asked-us.session).outbox)))
    print-line-uni ("asked-us-taught-nothing=" & show (((asked-us.session).arp).count == 0))
    print-line-uni ("survived")
  end
